#!/bin/sh
#
# Refuse a commit that changes behaviour without touching the documentation
# that describes it. AGENTS.md has said to do this from the beginning; saying
# it was not enough, so the check runs here where it cannot be skimmed past.
#
# Genuinely invisible work (an internal refactor, a typo in a test) has an
# escape hatch: git commit --no-verify.

staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR)
[ -z "$staged" ] && exit 0

behaviour=$(printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -E '^(client/src/|server/src/|supabase/migrations/)' | grep -vE '\.test\.ts$')
[ -z "$behaviour" ] && exit 0

docs=$(printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -E '^(PLAN\.md|README\.md|AGENTS\.md|CLAUDE\.md|\.env\.example)$')
[ -n "$docs" ] && exit 0

cat >&2 <<'MSG'

This commit changes behaviour but does not touch a single document.

PLAN.md is the product spec and it is authoritative: §0 for what is built or
deliberately deviating, §13 Phase 7 for what remains, the relevant spec section
when the rules themselves change, §14 when a change adds an acceptance
criterion. README.md covers the status paragraph, setup, scripts, environment
variables and the repository layout. AGENTS.md covers working conventions, and
.env.example every variable the code reads.

Stage the doc edit alongside the code and commit again.

If the change really is invisible to all of them, say so and skip the check:

    git commit --no-verify

MSG
exit 1
